Done in "[PATCH v5] jails/instances/lab-mail: Initial spec" [1]. It
adds an "alternatives considered" section that takes each decision in
turn: what the spec does, what it rejects, and why. It covers the
queuing MTA, the client-side queue (the reasoning above, nearly word
for word), the other relays, sender and recipient checks, imapc
against mbsync, IMAP against a shared mount, ip4 = inherit, the
ports, the single app password, and leaving your client out. Every
entry is a choice made in this thread or in "p7x mail relay jail";
none is new.

I will add the same section to any spec I write from now on.

> When posting new versions, also include an interdiff so I can clearly
> see the changes. I think interdiff is the right word.

Yes, that is the word. v5 has one below the diff, from git
format-patch --interdiff against v4. It covers the spec file only, so
the "Changes in v5" notes mention the new commit message paragraph. I
checked that git am still applies v5 to trunk with the interdiff in
place, and that the result matches my commit exactly.

> Good thinking. What's a good way to track these sorts of things? Is it a
> follow up in the spec? Add to todo.org? Or just leave it on the list and
> let a secretary bring it up later?

I suggest splitting the decision from the task.

- The decision goes in the spec. v5 records it as the last
  alternative: your client is left out, and sending as you takes one
  more allowed sender and an msmtp entry. A spec describes the end
  state, so a "follow-up" section would be a to-do list living in the
  wrong place.
- The task goes in todo.org, with the event that should trigger it:
  "when Emacs moves off the jj maildir, send through lab-mail as
  pat@patmaddox.com". That is the file you already work from.
- Do not rely on the list alone. A thread leaves the inbox once it
  has a reply, and nothing brings it back. A secretary would have to
  find the item again in the archive each time, and could not tell
  whether it was still open.

If that suits you, I will send a one-line todo.org patch.

